{"title":"All In the Mind with Sana Qadar - ABC listen","hostInformation":null,"description":"All in the Mind is an exploration of the mental: the mind, brain and behaviour — everything from addiction to artificial intelligence.","language":null,"targetAudience":{"interests":[],"ageGroups":[],"location":{"country":null,"state":null,"city":null}},"recommendationScenarios":[],"recommendationReasons":["Featured in ABC Most Listened"],"abcPodcastPage":"https://www.abc.net.au/listen/programs/allinthemind","imageUrl":"https://live-production.wcms.abc-cdn.net.au/ebd9fbda878c87d35b5f7e5adc662361?impolicy=wcms_crop_resize&cropH=3000&cropW=3000&xPos=0&yPos=0&width=862&height=862","applePodcastPage":null,"spotifyPodcastPage":null,"youtubePage":"https://www.youtube.com/abcaustralia","otherReviewLinks":[],"episodes":[{"title":"The psychology behind our moral outrage","description":"Politics, war, abortion, gun control — why do some topics make us so outraged? One theory is that our preoccupation with preventing harm is to blame.","publishedAt":null,"duration":"1777","audioUrl":null,"url":"https://www.abc.net.au/listen/programs/allinthemind/moral-outrage-psychology/106514274","transcript":null,"segments":[],"chapters":[],"highlights":[],"contentHash":null,"narrativeHook":null,"vibe":null,"entities":[],"contentLocations":[],"guests":[],"engagement":null},{"title":"Staying sane in space","description":"In the last couple of weeks, a lot of people have been getting excited about space exploration thanks to Artemis II.","publishedAt":null,"duration":"1746","audioUrl":null,"url":"https://www.abc.net.au/listen/programs/allinthemind/staying-sane-in-space-psychology/106488746","transcript":null,"segments":[],"chapters":[],"highlights":[],"contentHash":null,"narrativeHook":null,"vibe":null,"entities":[],"contentLocations":[],"guests":[],"engagement":null},{"title":"Mind Hacks with Dr Billy Garvey: \"being a bit kinder to myself\"","description":"Welcome back to All in the Mind's little sister: Mind Hacks.","publishedAt":null,"duration":"474","audioUrl":null,"url":"https://www.abc.net.au/listen/programs/allinthemind/mind-hacks-billy-garvey-kind-to-myself/106541800","transcript":null,"segments":[],"chapters":[],"highlights":[],"contentHash":null,"narrativeHook":null,"vibe":null,"entities":[],"contentLocations":[],"guests":[],"engagement":null},{"title":"Tantrums, sleep training and anxiety: Dr Billy Garvey answers your parenting questions","description":"How can I help my child with anxiety? What's the deal with sleep training? Are we supposed to ignore tantrums?","publishedAt":null,"duration":"1779","audioUrl":null,"url":"https://www.abc.net.au/listen/programs/allinthemind/billy-garvey-parenting-tantrums-sleep-anxiety/106462412","transcript":null,"segments":[],"chapters":[],"highlights":[],"contentHash":null,"narrativeHook":null,"vibe":null,"entities":[],"contentLocations":[],"guests":[],"engagement":null},{"title":"The psychology of masturbation","description":"This week we're talking about something intimate… and sometimes taboo: masturbation.","publishedAt":null,"duration":"1746","audioUrl":null,"url":"https://www.abc.net.au/listen/programs/allinthemind/brain-and-masturbation/106434336","transcript":null,"segments":[],"chapters":[],"highlights":[],"contentHash":null,"narrativeHook":null,"vibe":null,"entities":[],"contentLocations":[],"guests":[],"engagement":null},{"title":"Mind Hacks: Casey Beros","description":"Welcome back to All in the Mind's little sister: Mind Hacks.","publishedAt":null,"duration":null,"audioUrl":null,"url":"https://www.abc.net.au/listen/programs/allinthemind/mind-hacks-casey-beros/106492134","transcript":null,"segments":[],"chapters":[],"highlights":[],"contentHash":null,"narrativeHook":null,"vibe":null,"entities":[],"contentLocations":[],"guests":[],"engagement":null},{"title":"Tough conversations and anticipatory grief: being a carer","description":"When Casey Beros' dad was diagnosed with mesothelioma, she knew her entire world would change.","publishedAt":null,"duration":"1776","audioUrl":null,"url":"https://www.abc.net.au/listen/programs/allinthemind/tough-conversations-anticipatory-grief-carer/106409174","transcript":null,"segments":[],"chapters":[],"highlights":[],"contentHash":null,"narrativeHook":null,"vibe":null,"entities":[],"contentLocations":[],"guests":[],"engagement":null},{"title":"Family estrangement and infidelity: Esther Perel answers your questions","description":"How do you grieve a dying parent you're estranged from? Do cheaters ever change? People say love should feel 'easy', is that true?","publishedAt":null,"duration":"1806","audioUrl":null,"url":"https://www.abc.net.au/listen/programs/allinthemind/esther-perel-mailbag-estrangement-infidelity-love/106384146","transcript":null,"segments":[],"chapters":[],"highlights":[],"contentHash":null,"narrativeHook":null,"vibe":null,"entities":[],"contentLocations":[],"guests":[],"engagement":null},{"title":"Mind Hacks: Joel Pearson","description":"Welcome back to All in the Mind's little sister: Mind Hacks. In this episode, cognitive neuroscientist Joel Pearson tells us how he manages his own stress (clue: it involves saunas!)","publishedAt":null,"duration":"419","audioUrl":null,"url":"https://www.abc.net.au/listen/programs/allinthemind/mind-hacks-joel-pearson/106436678","transcript":null,"segments":[],"chapters":[],"highlights":[],"contentHash":null,"narrativeHook":null,"vibe":null,"entities":[],"contentLocations":[],"guests":[],"engagement":null},{"title":"Is AI making our brains lazier?","description":"Social media gave us brain rot; will AI give us cognitive atrophy? How can we possibly learn to cope with the sheer amount of uncertainty and change heading our way?","publishedAt":null,"duration":"1967","audioUrl":null,"url":"https://www.abc.net.au/listen/programs/allinthemind/ai-making-brains-lazier-psychology/106353300","transcript":null,"segments":[],"chapters":[],"highlights":[],"contentHash":null,"narrativeHook":null,"vibe":null,"entities":[],"contentLocations":[],"guests":[],"engagement":null}],"isPopular":true,"isAwardWinning":false,"averageRating":4.0,"ratingCount":50,"itunesId":73330911,"primaryGenre":"Life Sciences","appleGenres":["Life Sciences","Podcasts","Science","Health & Fitness"],"reviews":[{"author":"ItsTheAnd","rating":2.0,"content":"Those who shun conspiracy theorists are displaying their own narcissism “I just want that person back (for me)”. Instead of projecting yourself onto them, the other approach is to engage your friend and actually look at the documented peer-reviewed evidence of the growing failures, corruption and deception of government, intelligence agencies and media. For any compassionate person these dark trends are vital to bring to awareness and enact change upon. \n\nScience spokespeople during covid gaslighted the public into feeling guilt and shame for making the rational point that we have immune systems that easily handle a flu like virus with a 99.9% survival rate, especially if treated with cheap, common and safe antivirals. Though there is no money in that therefore no incentive to affirm non-profitable scientific rigour. \n\nThe government enforcement of an experimental vaccine that neither offered immunity nor safety fits the textbook definition of “abusive”. Lives have been destroyed not by a mild virus, but due to government overreach and the desperate ad-dollar funded media promoting a “safe and effective” treatment that was neither safe nor effective. Taxpayers will be paying off the covid debt decades into the future. “I was fine from my jab (therefore everyone else was too)” is pure narcissism and demonstrates low IQ all-or-nothing thinking. \n\nIt’s well established that the CIA propagated the term ‘conspiracy theorist’ and that they purposefully promote hoax conspiracies to muddy the waters, distract from the true and evidenced conspiracies and discredit “the conspiracy movement” (there is no movement). All of these proofs are in their own archives open to the public. \n\nThe solution to conspiracists is not to “save people” from realising truth according to your own standard of ignorance. It’s to boldly accept what the evidence is indicating, not live in wilful delusion. Vocalise your concern and solutions rationally, politely and hold yourself to a higher standard of intelligent inquiry. \n\nIf you’d like to understand how the world really works consider listening to “coffee with Scott adams” podcast and join others who’ve become aware of our societal and institutional deceptions and how to navigate truthfully in this increasingly 1984ish reality.","date":null},{"author":"Tate Causea","rating":1.0,"content":"Very disappointed in this episode. If I could give it zero I would. Biased is a word that applies here. \n\nTo suggest that “conspiracy theorists” attract a certain type of individual with a certain type of ‘mentality’ is nonsense. It’s also insulting, especially to people who bother to listen, read and research contentious subject matter. \n\nFor starters some conspiracies have turned out to be true, but lumping everyone together as conspiracy theorists is contempt prior to investigation. It’s prejudiced and judgemental. Having this attitude / approach is a much bigger problem than being a conspiracy theorist! It allows corruption to continue. \n\nSure some conspiracies are just total garbage, but some definitely aren’t. Anyone with an ability to reason and think critically can understand this and tell the difference. \n\nIt’s naive to think that scientific institutions, governments and every other set of power relations is going to be objective, transparent and honest. The reality is however that corruption exists everywhere, and it’s most commonly found in positions of power. “Power corrupts, and absolute power corrupts absolutely” as the quote goes.","date":null},{"author":"Mollydoggles","rating":3.0,"content":"There was a lot of emphasis on how horrible having BPD is from the mental health sufferers side but little mentioned on how destructive and horrible it is to be at the very mercy of those with BPD. \nMy workplace and my colleagues have suffered terribly from the utter ‘train wreck’ style that is working with a colleague with this diagnosis.From my experience there is very little understanding and empathy from those with BPD toward anyone else. And I DO NOT agree with that OT that the individual should conceal their diagnosis with management. \nHow else can a workplace plan ahead for times of hostility and violent mood swings of the Staff member with BPD?\nSorry but that was a reckless comment.\nPerhaps Sana, you could level the scales by having episodes that explore the experiences of those that live or work with someone with the multitude of mental health diagnoses you cover.\nCouldn’t hurt seeing the issue from both sides.\nSee them as bookends.","date":null},{"author":"zzzzz_1","rating":3.0,"content":"I really love this podcast. Sometimes it does brush over topics very broadly in a way that seemingly goes against its aims. \n\nThe episode on attachment does the exact thing the podcast is criticising social media users of doing. Allowing a researcher to say that the concept of mental load appeared only a few years ago in public consciousness through a French cartoon in a blog is absurd. This absolutely ignores the long history of Feminist, black, queer and revolutionary work in this space. \n\nBowlby was also not the first person to consider how the environment impacts a baby’s development - psychoanalysts had already been doing that for decades and Bowlby always acknowledged this. He was the first person to tie it to evolutionary biology.\n\nTwo very significant inconsistencies when you are claiming to set the record straight for all. \n\nReally love the podcast, but it is much more successful when it doesn’t have a snide tone.","date":null},{"author":"Trexmonst","rating":3.0,"content":"I get really concerned when “vanity” Is appealed to in order sell books. “High achievers” who chronically seek external validation and look at social media are in fact, garden variety insecure and anxious. It is INSUFFERABLE for a normal healthy actual high achiever ( without the vanity tag ) to be around people who whinge, compete look at social media like a teenager and seek praise. If your boss is a healthy person, your insecurity will reek ahead of you in any room you enter. And you will not become what your empty aspirations say you “should”. It’s pure vanity. Learn to self soothe. Learn to do a job without external gratification. Be quiet and get on with it without asking for prayer. Ughhh.","date":null}],"narrativeHook":"Exploring the limitlessness of the human mind and the mechanics of human behavior.","vibe":{"tone":["Curious","Deep"],"complexity":0.8,"pace":"Measured"},"originLocation":{"country":null,"state":null,"city":null},"geographicCoverage":[],"scoutingPriority":1.0}
